WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Today we start with sorting algorithms. Insertion sort and merge sort order a list of numbers; insertion sort needs quadratic time in the worst case, merge sort halves the list and merges the sorted halves in n log n time.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:01:26.000
Next is binary search. Binary search halves the search space of a sorted array in every step, so its running time is logarithmic and 1024 entries need at most 10 comparisons.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Let me take a detour into quantum computing. A quantum computer stores information in qubits that can be in superposition, and entanglement between qubits lets Shor's factoring procedure break RSA encryption much faster than classical machines.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Now graph traversal. Breadth-first search visits the vertices of a graph level by level using a queue, depth-first search follows a path to its end and backtracks using a stack.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:41.000
Last topic, red-black trees. A red-black tree is a balanced binary search tree where every node is coloured red or black, and rotations with recolouring keep the height logarithmic.
